00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:55.720
from a company called Dgraph.

00:01:55.720 --> 00:01:58.650
They have an open
source graph database

00:01:58.650 --> 00:02:01.420
that they write using GRPC.

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:04.070
And they talk about
actually how they go from--

00:02:04.070 --> 00:02:06.850
and originally using
just the go/rpc,

00:02:06.850 --> 00:02:09.310
so they use Go as
their primary language.

00:02:09.310 --> 00:02:11.610
But they ended up wanting
to replace it with GRPC.

00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:15.040
But the interesting part of
it is so GRPC uses protobuf.

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:16.540
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
By default. Indeed.

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:18.164
MARK MANDEL: By
default. Which is cool.

00:02:18.164 --> 00:02:20.069
But you can actually
switch it out.

00:02:20.069 --> 00:02:21.610
And because of
certain tradeoffs they

00:02:21.610 --> 00:02:24.270
wanted to make with sort of
message-side server sending

00:02:24.270 --> 00:02:26.460
and what they were doing
on both ends of the pipe,

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:29.070
Flat Buffers was a
better fit for them.

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:31.610
And so they talk about why
they made that decision

00:02:31.610 --> 00:02:33.480
and how they ended
up switching it out,

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:36.620
because in GRPC you can switch
out what your encoding type is,

00:02:36.620 --> 00:02:37.850
which is really awesome.

00:02:37.850 --> 00:02:39.600
And they sort of step
through all of that.

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:41.220
It's a really, really
interesting read

00:02:41.220 --> 00:02:43.039
