00:08:56.200 --> 00:08:59.649
And so that-- in addition to--
so Google Analytics gives you--

00:08:59.649 --> 00:09:01.940
you know, you can get the
source, the location of where

00:09:01.940 --> 00:09:03.960
people are coming
from, but it doesn't

00:09:03.960 --> 00:09:07.250
have things that are quite
custom to car dealers,

00:09:07.250 --> 00:09:10.790
say like the vehicle
model and the vehicle

00:09:10.790 --> 00:09:12.730
ID, or anything like that.

00:09:12.730 --> 00:09:14.690
So we use Google Tag
Manager to provide what's

00:09:14.690 --> 00:09:17.080
called custom dimensions.

00:09:17.080 --> 00:09:20.170
So on every page view
or click or event,

00:09:20.170 --> 00:09:23.060
we send through a
whole bunch of data

00:09:23.060 --> 00:09:26.140
all about what, say,
that vehicle is.

00:09:26.140 --> 00:09:28.080
That then gets sent
into Google Analytics,

00:09:28.080 --> 00:09:31.140
which we can then provide-- we
can use to provide insights on.

00:09:31.140 --> 00:09:34.470
So in addition to
basic web data,

00:09:34.470 --> 00:09:36.050
you can get more specific stuff.

00:09:36.050 --> 00:09:38.060
So Google Tag Manager
is really good,

00:09:38.060 --> 00:09:40.070
and really easily ties
into Google Analytics.

00:09:40.070 --> 00:09:41.820
LESTER LITCHFIELD:
Yeah, it's pretty cool.

00:09:41.820 --> 00:09:44.290
You can put a-- Google Tag
Manager works around a data

00:09:44.290 --> 00:09:47.400
layer, so this content
that on the client side

00:09:47.400 --> 00:09:50.316
you have this array or this
dictionary which just holds

00:09:50.316 --> 00:09:52.690
heaps of data about what's
going on at the time in, like,

00:09:52.690 --> 00:09:54.030
a key value store.

00:09:54.030 --> 00:09:58.710
So you have the name of the car
dealer, the color of the car,

00:09:58.710 --> 00:09:59.960
and all those sorts of things.

00:09:59.960 --> 00:10:02.530
You know, sometimes up to
30 or 40 different things.

00:10:02.530 --> 00:10:05.040
And then any actions they
happen on their page,

00:10:05.040 --> 00:10:08.610
like a click or a page
view or an interaction,

00:10:08.610 --> 00:10:10.320
you can pick which
ones of those you'd

00:10:10.320 --> 00:10:11.920
like to grab,
bundle that all up,

00:10:11.920 --> 00:10:13.840
and send it as a hit
to Google Analytics.

00:10:13.840 --> 00:10:18.953
So it's super flexible, and it
enables you to pretty easily

00:10:18.953 --> 00:10:21.070
pass pretty rich data around.

00:10:21.070 --> 00:10:22.040
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:10:22.040 --> 00:10:24.270
So basically, if I
understand correctly,

00:10:24.270 --> 00:10:27.360
what you're doing is you're
sending a bunch of information

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:30.410
to Google Analytics, you're
tagging it in different ways.

00:10:30.410 --> 00:10:31.870
What happens with it afterwards?

00:10:31.870 --> 00:10:33.040
How do you process it?

00:10:33.040 --> 00:10:35.666
LESTER LITCHFIELD: Well,
mainly you process it.

00:10:35.666 --> 00:10:36.889
[LAUGHTER]

00:10:36.889 --> 00:10:38.930
Then it's provided to us
in all these lovely kind

00:10:38.930 --> 00:10:40.280
of built-in reports.

00:10:40.280 --> 00:10:43.490
But increasingly now we're
starting to use integration

00:10:43.490 --> 00:10:44.834
with BigQuery.

00:10:44.834 --> 00:10:47.000
The reason that's really
cool is because it gives us

00:10:47.000 --> 00:10:48.650
access to the raw data.

00:10:48.650 --> 00:10:53.510
It's totally unsampled, and it
enables us to do kind of ad hoc

00:10:53.510 --> 00:10:57.630
or pretty complex queries
without having to really worry

00:10:57.630 --> 00:10:59.320
about compute power.

00:10:59.320 --> 00:11:00.970
We don't have the
resource internally

00:11:00.970 --> 00:11:04.730
to look over a year's weblogs
in a reasonable timeframe,

00:11:04.730 --> 00:11:05.830
but you guys do.

00:11:05.830 --> 00:11:07.630
So that's sort of
how we're using it.

00:11:07.630 --> 00:11:09.130
And Emily can talk
a little bit more

00:11:09.130 --> 00:11:11.960
about how she's been using
the BigQuery API, which

00:11:11.960 --> 00:11:14.310
is really cool, the fact
that that massive, massive

00:11:14.310 --> 00:11:17.120
datastore, where it's about
like 30, 40 gigs a day

00:11:17.120 --> 00:11:20.470
of logs we export to BigQuery
is all available right

00:11:20.470 --> 00:11:23.210
behind an API for her
to be able to kind

00:11:23.210 --> 00:11:26.539
of consume and aggregate
or expose on the website.

00:11:26.539 --> 00:11:28.830
MARK MANDEL: I'll take you
back a step just real quick,

00:11:28.830 --> 00:11:29.260
as well.

00:11:29.260 --> 00:11:31.100
Do you want to talk a
little bit about what

00:11:31.100 --> 00:11:34.006
does that process look like
for taking Google Analytics

00:11:34.006 --> 00:11:35.130
and pushing it to BigQuery?

00:11:35.130 --> 00:11:37.820
Is it like just a checkbox,
or is there a lot of work?

00:11:37.820 --> 00:11:39.530
Like, what had to happen there?

00:11:39.530 --> 00:11:41.320
LESTER LITCHFIELD: It was
sort of part of the GAP deal,

00:11:41.320 --> 00:11:41.620
really.

00:11:41.620 --> 00:11:42.850
It was offered to
us as a feature,

00:11:42.850 --> 00:11:44.933
and we thought, oh, yeah,
that kind of looks cool.

00:11:44.933 --> 00:11:48.260
So we said yeah,
go ahead, do that.

00:11:48.260 --> 00:11:50.390
So they set up a
cloud project for us.

00:11:50.390 --> 00:11:53.204
And then a little bit
later, we were like, hey,

00:11:53.204 --> 00:11:54.120
we should have a look.

00:11:54.120 --> 00:11:55.710
And when we had a look,
we were like, wow,

00:11:55.710 --> 00:11:56.830
this is pretty impressive.

00:11:56.830 --> 00:11:59.470
Not only is there heaps of
data there-- and especially

00:11:59.470 --> 00:12:01.990
with the custom dimension
stuff, it's really rich--

00:12:01.990 --> 00:12:06.020
but also the processing
power is not like anything

00:12:06.020 --> 00:12:07.500
we've seen before.

00:12:07.500 --> 00:12:09.000
It's sort of the
power of Grayskull,

00:12:09.000 --> 00:12:10.208
really, when you [INAUDIBLE].

00:12:10.208 --> 00:12:10.794
[LAUGHTER]

00:12:10.794 --> 00:12:12.335
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I
got the reference.

00:12:12.335 --> 00:12:13.293
I'm so proud right now.

00:12:13.293 --> 00:12:15.022
MARK MANDEL: You
mentioned a GAP deal.

00:12:15.022 --> 00:12:16.230
I don't know what that means.

00:12:16.230 --> 00:12:17.467
Do you want to explain that?

00:12:17.467 --> 00:12:19.300
LESTER LITCHFIELD:
Google Analytics Premium.

00:12:19.300 --> 00:12:21.870
So it's just the difference
between the free one, which

00:12:21.870 --> 00:12:26.020
is you have a lot of
your data sampled down

00:12:26.020 --> 00:12:27.560
for obvious reasons
that, you know,

00:12:27.560 --> 00:12:29.592
when half the web is
using it for free,

00:12:29.592 --> 00:12:31.800
it's pretty hard to provide
loads of processing power

00:12:31.800 --> 00:12:33.937
for that, to the paid version.

00:12:33.937 --> 00:12:36.270
So the paid version gives you
kind of unsampled reports,

00:12:36.270 --> 00:12:39.210
and also these integrations
into BigQuery and other kind

00:12:39.210 --> 00:12:40.749
of premium features.

00:12:40.749 --> 00:12:43.165
EMILY MELHUISH: I think that
the best way Lester described

00:12:43.165 --> 00:12:47.435
it a few months back was that
Google Analytics was for kids,

00:12:47.435 --> 00:12:49.740
and then BigQuery was for
the grown-ups, because it's

00:12:49.740 --> 00:12:53.340
like the real, raw data in
there that you've got to query.

00:12:53.340 --> 00:12:55.624
MARK MANDEL: So yeah,
Emily, Lester was sort of

00:12:55.624 --> 00:12:57.290
sending it over to
you to talk about how

00:12:57.290 --> 00:12:58.477
you use the BigQuery API.

00:12:58.477 --> 00:13:00.310
Do you want to talk a
little bit about that?

00:13:00.310 --> 00:13:01.434
EMILY MELHUISH: Yeah, sure.

00:13:01.434 --> 00:13:05.470
So when we first started with
BigQuery, it was kind of new,

00:13:05.470 --> 00:13:07.230
and there wasn't much
documentation on how

00:13:07.230 --> 00:13:09.000
people were using it.

00:13:09.000 --> 00:13:13.956
So we spent a lot of time trying
to figure out how to use it.

00:13:13.956 --> 00:13:15.580
Eventually, of course,
thankfully there

00:13:15.580 --> 00:13:18.380
is an awesome API
that we can use.

00:13:18.380 --> 00:13:20.855
But obviously, the awesome
thing about BigQuery

00:13:20.855 --> 00:13:25.100
is you can run massive queries
in a few seconds' time.

00:13:25.100 --> 00:13:28.830
However, we were looking at
providing business insights

00:13:28.830 --> 00:13:30.210
for our customers,
and we sort of

00:13:30.210 --> 00:13:35.310
wanted a dashboard to load
within a few microseconds as

00:13:35.310 --> 00:13:37.310
opposed to-- or
milliseconds as opposed to,

00:13:37.310 --> 00:13:40.397
you know, five seconds that--
as a data analytics person,

00:13:40.397 --> 00:13:41.980
you wouldn't mind,
but customers might

00:13:41.980 --> 00:13:44.963
get frustrated having to wait
for data to come through.

00:13:44.963 --> 00:13:47.320
And because BigQuery,
I believe, have, like,

00:13:47.320 --> 00:13:49.820
a one-second minimum query.

00:13:49.820 --> 00:13:53.290
So as we have so much
data in BigQuery,

00:13:53.290 --> 00:13:56.820
we had to figure out a way
to get what we wanted out,

00:13:56.820 --> 00:14:00.340
and be able to query it
quickly and get the data

00:14:00.340 --> 00:14:02.640
to our customers, basically.

00:14:02.640 --> 00:14:06.610
So for that, what we did is
we set up a scheduled app

00:14:06.610 --> 00:14:07.850
with the BigQuery API.

00:14:07.850 --> 00:14:10.980
And basically every day, we'd
go to the massive table, which

00:14:10.980 --> 00:14:14.550
I think is about
3.3 million rows,

00:14:14.550 --> 00:14:17.910
and we basically
query, OK, give me

00:14:17.910 --> 00:14:20.630
all of these people's
listings on Trade Me,

00:14:20.630 --> 00:14:22.290
and give me all of the data.

00:14:22.290 --> 00:14:26.450
And we export that
into our own database.

00:14:26.450 --> 00:14:30.120
And then from there, we
can have our web app talk

00:14:30.120 --> 00:14:33.460
to our database and pull out
the data very, very quickly.

00:14:33.460 --> 00:14:36.240
So that was a pretty
good way to do it.

00:14:36.240 --> 00:14:38.420
And it means we can run
the scheduled app at, say,

00:14:38.420 --> 00:14:39.480
3:00 AM in the morning.

00:14:39.480 --> 00:14:43.100
It'll be done in about, I
think, 30 seconds, usually.

00:14:43.100 --> 00:14:47.030
And usually we're adding,
say, about 30 gig of data.

00:14:47.030 --> 00:14:49.940
So every day, to get all of
the listings that we need,

00:14:49.940 --> 00:14:53.160
it's about 30 gig
of data putting

00:14:53.160 --> 00:14:55.460
into it, which is quite a bit.

00:14:55.460 --> 00:14:58.079
And I think when I
was-- I usually, like,

00:14:58.079 --> 00:15:00.120
play around with things
to get the queries right.

00:15:00.120 --> 00:15:04.970
And I think the best query I
got was 480 gigabits of data

00:15:04.970 --> 00:15:05.580
to be queried.

00:15:05.580 --> 00:15:08.330
And I think it did it in, like,
45 seconds or something crazy.

00:15:08.330 --> 00:15:09.140
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:15:09.140 --> 00:15:10.570
EMILY MELHUISH: That
was pretty awesome.

00:15:10.570 --> 00:15:11.700
MARK MANDEL: So
basically, from what

00:15:11.700 --> 00:15:13.720
I understand, you're
basically pulling down

00:15:13.720 --> 00:15:15.270
aggregate information
from BigQuery?

00:15:15.270 --> 00:15:16.650
Would that be pretty accurate?

00:15:16.650 --> 00:15:17.270
EMILY MELHUISH: Yeah.

00:15:17.270 --> 00:15:18.120
LESTER LITCHFIELD: Yeah.

00:15:18.120 --> 00:15:18.840
EMILY MELHUISH: Definitely.

00:15:18.840 --> 00:15:19.660
LESTER LITCHFIELD:
Yeah, because it's

00:15:19.660 --> 00:15:20.990
got that level of granularity.

00:15:20.990 --> 00:15:23.590
And sometimes you want to
go deep into that nesting.

00:15:23.590 --> 00:15:24.480
It's really
interesting, actually,

00:15:24.480 --> 00:15:26.960
coming from a SQL background
and doing a lot of SQL queries

00:15:26.960 --> 00:15:29.100
where you go-- you know, the
longer you go down your table,

00:15:29.100 --> 00:15:30.480
the longer your query takes.

00:15:30.480 --> 00:15:32.050
But going down in
BigQuery is easy.

00:15:32.050 --> 00:15:35.120
It's going-- right?-- so
increasing down the levels

00:15:35.120 --> 00:15:36.710
of nesting.

00:15:36.710 --> 00:15:39.540
So I'm still not really sure
whether the BigQuery table

00:15:39.540 --> 00:15:42.590
structure is SQL
or non-SQL-like,

00:15:42.590 --> 00:15:47.634
because it gives you a tabular
format, but it's nested

00:15:47.634 --> 00:15:49.050
and you've got a
lot of-- it looks

00:15:49.050 --> 00:15:51.760
almost sparse because
you've got a lot of nulls.

00:15:51.760 --> 00:15:55.980
So it's some kind of NoSQL
sparsity-cum-tabular data

00:15:55.980 --> 00:15:56.480
format.

00:15:56.480 --> 00:15:59.350
And there's a few tricks to kind
of being able to pull up data

00:15:59.350 --> 00:16:01.910
from the lowest level of
nesting to the highest, which

00:16:01.910 --> 00:16:03.960
had been fun to learn about.

00:16:03.960 --> 00:16:05.482
But yeah, it works brilliantly.

00:16:05.482 --> 00:16:07.440
MARK MANDEL: So Lester,
I'm quite curious, too.

00:16:07.440 --> 00:16:08.690
Like, you're a data scientist.

00:16:08.690 --> 00:16:13.050
How has having access to all
that data inside BigQuery

00:16:13.050 --> 00:16:16.320
changed your job in terms of
how you interact with Trade

00:16:16.320 --> 00:16:18.120
Me and the data it provides?

00:16:18.120 --> 00:16:19.830
LESTER LITCHFIELD:
Well previously, we

00:16:19.830 --> 00:16:22.450
would be able to get very
limited numbers of answers.

00:16:22.450 --> 00:16:26.130
We'd go, oh, is your
question addressable

00:16:26.130 --> 00:16:27.250
by one of our metrics?

00:16:27.250 --> 00:16:29.670
And sometimes, we'd have to
get a half answer by, like,

00:16:29.670 --> 00:16:32.730
doing, like, 30 queries
and joining them up.

00:16:32.730 --> 00:16:35.710
But now, like, pretty much
with Google Tag Manager,

00:16:35.710 --> 00:16:39.310
every click is basically
available to be tracked.

00:16:39.310 --> 00:16:43.090
And with BigQuery, any kind
of condition or process,

00:16:43.090 --> 00:16:45.330
or basically anything, is there.

00:16:45.330 --> 00:16:47.220
So it takes the
pressure off the data,

00:16:47.220 --> 00:16:49.270
and means that
answering the questions

00:16:49.270 --> 00:16:53.400
is about how good are you as
a data scientist at framing

00:16:53.400 --> 00:16:57.992
the question and bringing
through the answers in a kind

00:16:57.992 --> 00:17:00.200
of clear and-- so it puts
the pressure on the science

00:17:00.200 --> 00:17:01.460
rather than the data.

00:17:01.460 --> 00:17:02.980
EMILY MELHUISH:
It's also very easy

00:17:02.980 --> 00:17:08.810
to have a slight mishap
somewhere in the data.

00:17:08.810 --> 00:17:12.730
And if that happens, a
lot of your reporting

00:17:12.730 --> 00:17:14.316
does some interesting things.

00:17:14.316 --> 00:17:20.859
So in our scheduled app,
which basically queries

00:17:20.859 --> 00:17:24.730
all of the BigQuery data,
what we actually had to set up

00:17:24.730 --> 00:17:28.480
was a lot of sort of like tests
to check that the data was

00:17:28.480 --> 00:17:31.090
still coming through correctly.

00:17:31.090 --> 00:17:32.620
So that was quite cool.

00:17:32.620 --> 00:17:36.060
And the BigQuery API allows
us to do that pretty easily,

00:17:36.060 --> 00:17:39.360
and has quite good logging
and everything for that.

00:17:39.360 --> 00:17:40.772
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:17:40.772 --> 00:17:44.520
Also, Lester, you mentioned
you do a lot of AB testing.

00:17:44.520 --> 00:17:47.230
I know Google Analytics has some
system tools and capabilities

00:17:47.230 --> 00:17:48.090
built into that.

00:17:48.090 --> 00:17:50.307
Is that changed as well
within BigQuery, now

00:17:50.307 --> 00:17:52.390
that you have that power,
or is it still the same?

00:17:52.390 --> 00:17:53.280
How are you doing that?

00:17:53.280 --> 00:17:55.780
LESTER LITCHFIELD: We sometimes
use Google Analytics content

00:17:55.780 --> 00:17:56.730
experiments.

00:17:56.730 --> 00:17:59.240
And they work really well in
a sort of a certain scope.

00:17:59.240 --> 00:18:01.560
Like my favorite thing
is that it wraps some,

00:18:01.560 --> 00:18:04.367
like, on further reading,
pretty meaty Bayesian stats

00:18:04.367 --> 00:18:06.242
in a really nice wrapper,
so you don't really

00:18:06.242 --> 00:18:07.480
have to think about it.

00:18:07.480 --> 00:18:08.980
Unfortunately,
sometimes you do have

00:18:08.980 --> 00:18:12.350
to think about it because
the framework doesn't quite

00:18:12.350 --> 00:18:14.040
fit for the question you have.

00:18:14.040 --> 00:18:15.680
And the cool thing
about BigQuery

00:18:15.680 --> 00:18:17.870
is, like, we use Python
quite a lot internally,

00:18:17.870 --> 00:18:21.410
and so I can go into Python,
pull out some aggregates

00:18:21.410 --> 00:18:25.640
from BigQuery, and then run any
kind of statistical processing

00:18:25.640 --> 00:18:33.180
over that from, say, [INAUDIBLE]
or SciPy or any of those kind

00:18:33.180 --> 00:18:37.120
of big scientific libraries.

00:18:37.120 --> 00:18:40.410
So it means that yeah, you
get the freedom to kind of do

00:18:40.410 --> 00:18:43.154
AB testing however you
want based on the data.

00:18:43.154 --> 00:18:44.070
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:18:44.070 --> 00:18:46.040
So I was wondering,
at some point

00:18:46.040 --> 00:18:49.730
you were talking about the fact
that the way you use BigQuery,

00:18:49.730 --> 00:18:53.510
since BigQuery has
that one-second minimum

00:18:53.510 --> 00:18:56.880
for a request, what you do
is you store some aggregates

00:18:56.880 --> 00:19:00.002
into some temporary database.

00:19:00.002 --> 00:19:01.960
Could you tell us a little
bit more about that?

00:19:01.960 --> 00:19:03.970
Where is that database running?

00:19:03.970 --> 00:19:06.330
What kind of database is this?

00:19:06.330 --> 00:19:07.710
EMILY MELHUISH: Yeah, sure.

00:19:07.710 --> 00:19:12.340
So for us here at
Trade Me, we have

00:19:12.340 --> 00:19:15.490
quite a lot of different
databases everywhere.

00:19:15.490 --> 00:19:20.220
So we do have one big
database that stores basically

00:19:20.220 --> 00:19:24.030
like the last day's data.

00:19:24.030 --> 00:19:25.720
So what happens
is because there's

00:19:25.720 --> 00:19:30.120
a bit of delay between getting
everything flowing from Trade

00:19:30.120 --> 00:19:32.140
Me to Google Tag Manager
to Google Analytics

00:19:32.140 --> 00:19:34.470
then into BigQuery,
and then [INAUDIBLE]

00:19:34.470 --> 00:19:39.780
to query that, we usually get,
like, the last-- yesterday's

00:19:39.780 --> 00:19:40.840
data.

00:19:40.840 --> 00:19:44.440
And so what happens is that
all gets-- so in BigQuery,

00:19:44.440 --> 00:19:47.330
each day is a new table.

00:19:47.330 --> 00:19:54.010
And so what happens each day for
us is we append each day's data

00:19:54.010 --> 00:19:55.450
to our database.

00:19:55.450 --> 00:19:58.880
And we only hold about 90
days of data at a time.

00:19:58.880 --> 00:20:03.180
So basically, because
for business insights

00:20:03.180 --> 00:20:06.150
we don't report back
on 90 days, obviously,

00:20:06.150 --> 00:20:08.520
but if someone,
say, wants to report

00:20:08.520 --> 00:20:10.310
on a year's worth
of data, what we do

00:20:10.310 --> 00:20:13.350
is we then go to BigQuery,
since the data is already there.

00:20:13.350 --> 00:20:16.450
So basically, we effectively
cache 90 days of data.

00:20:16.450 --> 00:20:20.890
And then, in BigQuery,
if we want more data,

00:20:20.890 --> 00:20:23.520
they just have to wait
more time to sort of get

00:20:23.520 --> 00:20:27.040
90 days plus whatever
more of data.

00:20:27.040 --> 00:20:30.570
So that's quite good
for us to store the data

00:20:30.570 --> 00:20:33.110
that we want immediately.

00:20:33.110 --> 00:20:37.400
But then if they want
more, they can take more.

00:20:37.400 --> 00:20:40.620
Sorry, they're in MySQL tables.

00:20:40.620 --> 00:20:43.760
MARK MANDEL: I'm curious, do
you run your own infrastructure?

00:20:43.760 --> 00:20:46.850
I don't know how good the cloud
providers are in New Zealand.

00:20:46.850 --> 00:20:48.880
How does that,
like, locality work?

00:20:48.880 --> 00:20:49.220
LESTER LITCHFIELD: Sure.

00:20:49.220 --> 00:20:50.720
Yeah, we run our
own infrastructure.

00:20:50.720 --> 00:20:51.820
So we have boxes.

00:20:51.820 --> 00:20:52.390
Yeah.

00:20:52.390 --> 00:20:53.730
Remember those days.

00:20:53.730 --> 00:20:55.050
[LAUGHTER]

00:20:55.050 --> 00:20:58.530
And yeah, we have
a ton of boxes.

00:20:58.530 --> 00:21:01.770
We're not sort of super
distributed as a model.

00:21:01.770 --> 00:21:06.320
Like we sort of-- we're largely
on a kind of Microsoft stack.

00:21:06.320 --> 00:21:08.650
So this is where it's
quite interesting for us,

00:21:08.650 --> 00:21:13.530
even beyond analytics, when you
get into the data processing

00:21:13.530 --> 00:21:18.060
where the distributed models
start to make a lot of sense,

00:21:18.060 --> 00:21:22.860
that we may begin to [INAUDIBLE]
looking into how the Google

00:21:22.860 --> 00:21:26.140
Cloud Platform can help provide
some of that distributed

00:21:26.140 --> 00:21:27.352
kind of processing power.

00:21:27.352 --> 00:21:28.810
MARK MANDEL: So
I'm guessing you've

00:21:28.810 --> 00:21:31.640
got local machines, because
latency, and because, you know,

00:21:31.640 --> 00:21:33.990
